{
  "policy_id": "con-88-require-developer-funded-cladding-remediation",
  "party": "Conservative",
  "policy_title": "Require Developer-Funded Cladding Remediation",
  "outcome": "O2",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O2",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "This policy shields leaseholders from remediation bills that can exceed \u00a3100,000, directly protecting their disposable income. Progress is slow and some costs like higher insurance premiums remain, so the benefit is real but incomplete.",
    "biggest_unknown": "How quickly remediation actually completes \u2014 slow progress means leaseholders face ongoing insurance cost burdens and restricted ability to sell for years longer.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy requires continuation of developer-funded remediation programmes for mid- and high-rise buildings, supporting affected leaseholders.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "requiring the continuation of developer-funded remediation programmes for mid- and high-rise buildings"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Qualifying leaseholders in buildings above 11 metres are legally protected from the costs of cladding remediation work.",
        "evidence_id": "E1",
        "quote": "Qualifying leaseholders in buildings above 11 metres are now legally protected from the costs of cladding remediation work"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Remediation invoices have in some cases exceeded \u00a3100,000 per leaseholder \u2014 the scale of financial exposure this policy addresses.",
        "evidence_id": "E3",
        "quote": "hugely reduce invoices sent to leaseholders for taking down cladding, which in some cases have exceeded \u00a3100,000"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Leaseholders face ongoing financial strain from increased insurance premiums and difficulties selling their properties.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "Leaseholders also face ongoing financial strain from increased insurance premiums and difficulties selling their properties, particularly due to delays in remediation or issues with non-cladding defects"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "More than 50 developers have signed a legally binding remediation contract committing to fund and fix fire safety defects.",
        "evidence_id": "E11",
        "quote": "This commitment is legally binding and requires them to fund and undertake necessary remediation, as well as reimburse taxpayers for funding already spent on their buildings"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Remediation progress is considered far too slow, with completion of all buildings not expected by the 2029 target.",
        "evidence_id": "E19",
        "quote": "remediation works twelve years after the Grenfell fire still not expected to be complete for all buildings by the 2029 target"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Many mid-rise and low-rise buildings remain untouched by remediation.",
        "evidence_id": "E20",
        "quote": "Many mid-rise and low-rise buildings remain untouched"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The core O2 question is whether leaseholders can afford essentials \u2014 and the cladding crisis has imposed ruinous costs on them, with individual bills sometimes exceeding \u00a3100,000. By legally requiring developers to fund remediation (rather than passing costs to leaseholders), this policy directly protects the disposable incomes of affected households. That mechanism is already enacted and partially in force, not merely aspirational: developers are legally bound, and leaseholders above 11m are already protected. That earns 'improves'. The magnitude is moderate rather than major for two reasons. First, the protection only fully applies above 11m; many mid-rise and lower buildings remain untouched. Second, while remediation bills are removed, other ongoing financial pressures \u2014 higher insurance premiums, restricted mortgage and resale markets \u2014 persist as real costs that this policy does not directly address. Third, the pace of actual remediation is widely criticised as far too slow, meaning leaseholders remain in financial and practical limbo for longer than the policy's headline promise implies. There is no counterfactual evidence that absent this policy developers would voluntarily fund remediation at scale \u2014 the legal compulsion is the instrument, and it has secured over 50 developer signatories. The improvement is therefore genuinely additional. Confidence is moderate: the legal protections are real and in force, but delivery risk and scope gaps (lower-rise buildings, insurance costs) prevent a high-confidence 'major' rating.",
